<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fraser River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fraser River is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a>, flowing from the Rocky Mountains to the Pacific near Vancouver. The discovery of gold on its lower course in 1858 drew some 30,000 miners, mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, turning Victoria into a boom town and prompting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to create the Colony of British Columbia that year to keep control of the mainland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fraser-river.jpg" alt="Fraser River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M. Lounsbery · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FraserRiver.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
